#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199525 :tutorial/content "Let $T_P$ and $T_Q$ be the truth sets of the predicates $P(x_1,x_2,\\ldots,x_n)$ and $Q(x_1,x_2,\\ldots,x_n),$ respectively. Note that we're assuming our predicates have the same universal set.\n\nThe **implication** of the predicates $P$ and $Q$ is a new predicate that we denote as follows:\n\n$$\nP(x_1,x_2,\\ldots,x_n) \\Rightarrow Q(x_1,x_2,\\ldots,x_n)\n$$\n\nThis implication is false whenever $P(x_1,x_2,\\ldots,x_n)$ is true *and* $Q(x_1,x_2,\\ldots,x_n)$ is false for a specific set of respective variable assignments $x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_n.$\n\nNow, recall the following logical equivalence:\n\n$$\nP\\Rightarrow Q \\equiv \\lnot P \\lor Q\n$$\n\nTherefore, the truth set of $P \\Rightarrow Q$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}_{P⇒Q} & ={T}_{¬P∨Q} \\\\  & ={T}_{¬P}\\cup {T}_{Q} \\\\  & ={\\overset{―}{T}}_{P}\\cup {T}_{Q}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor example, suppose we have the following universal set:\n\n$$\nU = \\big\\{ 1,2,3,4,5,6,7,8,9 \\big\\}\n$$\n\nLet's define the following predicates over $U{:}$\n\n- $P(x):$ $x$ is prime\n\n- $Q(x):$ $9$ is divisible by $x$\n\nTo find the truth set of $P(x) \\Rightarrow Q(x),$ we note the following:\n\n- The truth set of $P(x)$ is $T_P = \\left\\{ 2,3,5,7 \\right\\}.$ As a result, the complement is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\overset{―}{T}}_{P}=\\overset{―}{\\{2,3,5,7\\}}=\\{1,4,6,8,9\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The truth set of $Q(x)$ is \n\n$$\nT_Q = \\big\\{ 1,3,9 \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}_{P⇒Q} & ={\\overset{―}{T}}_{P}\\cup {T}_{Q} \\\\  & =\\{1,4,6,8,9\\}\\cup \\{1,3,9\\} \\\\  & =\\{1,3,4,6,8,9\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "06a295ff-82fc-5afe-8aa1-b24421f2c474" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7992 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199526 :tutorial/content "Since implication and equivalence of predicates are simply generalizations of logical operations on statements, all the properties of these operations can be translated to the corresponding operations on predicates.\n\nIn particular, for predicates $P(x_1,x_2\\ldots,x_n)$ and $Q(y_1,y_2\\ldots,y_m),$ we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P⇒Q & ≡¬P∨Q \\\\ P⇔Q & ≡(P⇒Q)∧(Q⇒P) \\\\  & ≡(¬P∨Q)∧(¬Q∨P) \\\\  & ≡(¬P∧¬Q)∨(P∧Q)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's remind ourselves of the grammatical constructions for implications and biconditionals that we've encountered so far:\n\nFor conditional statements, the following are equivalent:\n\n- $P\\Rightarrow Q$\n\n- *If $P,$ then $Q$*\n\n- *$P$ implies $Q$*\n\nFor biconditional statements, the following are equivalent:\n\n- $P\\Leftrightarrow Q$\n\n- *$P$ is equivalent to $Q$*\n\n- *$P$ if and only if $Q$*\n\n- *If $P$ then $Q,$ and if $Q$ then $P$*" :tutorial/id #uuid "599dc11e-cbd2-596c-bb72-4f73b83034ab" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7993 :tutorial/title "Logical Laws Involving Implications and Equivalences With Predicates"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199527 :tutorial/content "Let $T_P$ and $T_Q$ be the truth sets of the predicates $P(x_1,x_2,\\ldots,x_n)$ and $Q(x_1,x_2,\\ldots,x_n),$ respectively. Again, we're assuming our predicates have the same universal set.\n\nThe **equivalence** of the predicates $P$ and $Q$ is a new predicate that we denote as follows:\n\n$$\nP(x_1,x_2,\\ldots,x_n) \\Leftrightarrow Q(x_1,x_2,\\ldots,x_n)\n$$\n\nThis predicate is true when $P$ and $Q$ have the same truth values for a specific set of variable assignments $x_1,x_2,\\ldots, x_n.$\n\n$$\nP \\Leftrightarrow Q \\equiv \\big(P \\land Q\\big) \\lor \\big(\\neg P \\land \\neg Q\\big)\n$$\n\nLet's look at some examples. Assume $x\\in\\mathbb R$ throughout.\n\n- The following statement is an equivalence (i.e., it is true).\n\n$$\nx + 2 = 5 \\quad\\Leftrightarrow\\quad x=3\n$$\n\nThis statement is true because \n\n$$\n\\left(x+2=5\\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad x=3\\right)\\qquad\\textrm{and}\\qquad \\left(x=3\\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad x+2 = 5\\right).\n$$\n\n- The following statement is **not** an equivalence (i.e., it is false).\n\n$$\nx^2 = 4 \\quad\\Leftrightarrow\\quad x=2\n$$\n\nThis statement is false because \n\n$$\n\\left(x=2 \\quad \\Rightarrow\\quad  x^2 = 4\\right) \\qquad \\textrm{and} \\qquad \\left(x^2=4\\quad \\not\\Rightarrow\\quad x=2\\right).\n$$\n\nHowever, the following statement is true:\n\n$$\nx^2 = 4 \\quad\\Leftrightarrow\\quad x=\\pm 2\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "5e9f21f0-8dbb-5442-968e-b1fb4425fef7" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7995 :tutorial/title "Equivalence of Predicates"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199603 :tutorial/content "For predicates $P(x_1,x_2,\\ldots,x_n)$ and $Q(x_1,x_2,\\ldots,x_n),$ the equivalence $P(x_1,x_2,\\ldots,x_n)\\Leftrightarrow Q(x_1,x_2,\\ldots,x_n)$ is true when $P$ and $Q$ have the same truth value.  \n\n$$\nP \\Leftrightarrow Q \\equiv \\big(P \\land Q\\big) \\lor \\big(\\neg P \\land \\neg Q\\big)\n$$\n\nIt follows that the truth set of $P \\Leftrightarrow Q$ can be found using\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}_{P⇔Q}=({T}_{P}\\cap {T}_{Q})\\cup ({\\overset{―}{T}}_{P}\\cap {\\overset{―}{T}}_{Q}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's find the truth set of $P(x) \\Leftrightarrow Q(x)$ given the following predicates defined over the universal set $\\mathbb{R}{:}$\n\n- $P(x):$ $1-5x  < 11$\n\n- $Q(x):$ $x \\geq -1$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- To find the the truth set of $P(x),$ we solve the corresponding inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-5x & <11 \\\\ -5x & <10 \\\\ x & >-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the truth set of $P(x)$ is $T_P = (-2, \\infty)$ and its complement is $\\overline{T}_P = (-\\infty,-2].$\n\n- The truth set of $Q(x)$ is $T_Q = [-1, \\infty)$ and its complement is $\\overline{T}_Q = (-\\infty,-1).$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}_{P}\\cap {T}_{Q} & =(-2,\\infty )\\cap [-1,\\infty ) \\\\  & =[-1,\\infty ) \\\\ {\\overset{―}{T}}_{P}\\cap {\\overset{―}{T}}_{Q} & =(-\\infty ,-2]\\cap (-\\infty ,-1) \\\\  & =(-\\infty ,-2] \\\\ {T}_{P⇔Q} & =({T}_{P}\\cap {T}_{Q})\\cup ({\\overset{―}{T}}_{P}\\cap {\\overset{―}{T}}_{Q}) \\\\  & =[-1,\\infty )\\cup (-\\infty ,-2] \\\\  & =(-\\infty ,-2]\\cup [-1,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's see another example." :tutorial/id #uuid "196ed409-81bb-5e86-bf1c-5e34aa4fb041" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9146 :tutorial/title "Truth Sets of Equivalences"}]}
